\fs28 \cf2 \cb5 I\'92ll test that exact client combination against the corrected Short and the calibrated long-form interview. If format 18 comes through, I\'92ll extract 16 kHz mono audio and compare the stronger Whisper outputs against your hand corrections before processing the rest.\cb1 \
\cb5 That worked. Both the 58-second Short and the full 48-minute interview downloaded with intact AAC audio, so the media-access blocker is resolved. I\'92m moving ahead with the calibration comparison now.\cb1 \
\cb5 The calibration is already useful: large-v3 correctly recovers substantially more structure than the original small-model text, but it still mangles isolated proper names when music is under the speech. Your corrections supply those names and recurring phrases. I\'92ll therefore use an ensemble pass\'97large-v3 plus large-v3-turbo, constrained by the corrected vocabulary\'97and manually reconcile only the disagreements rather than trusting either raw output wholesale.\cb1 \
\cb5 The client-switch is holding across the batch. TikTok media is also downloading directly, so the audit can cover the previously uncorrected TikTok section as requested. I\'92m keeping the human-corrected YouTube material untouched and using it only as a vocabulary and punctuation benchmark.\cb1 \
\cb5 The first full comparison has already found material errors, not just punctuation: one TikTok was reduced from roughly 160 words to 17, another from about 640 words to 28, \'93Mayan mythology\'94 became \'93mind mythology,\'94 \'93Realm\'94 became \'93wrap,\'94 and several names were corrupted. I\'92m sending the heavily disputed clips through the slower full large-v3 model now; the cleaner clips do not need that extra pass.\cb1 \
\cb5 The audit is now separating two kinds of problems: ordinary word substitutions, which are easy to repair, and catastrophic omissions/repetition artifacts, which require replacing whole transcript blocks. The latter are concentrated in a few TikToks and several long-form passages; I\'92m preserving the existing speaker labels while testing the spoken content independently.\cb1 \
